On the roof of a downtown hotel in Riverton, between the air-conditioning units and a row of potted herbs, sit four white wooden boxes. They hold roughly two hundred thousand honeybees, which the hotel's chef inspects every Tuesday morning. Urban hives often produce {{1|less}} honey than rural ones, but the chef is not trying to compete with farms. If the chef {{2|was}} to skip an inspection during the spring swarming season, half of a colony might leave for a nearby tree.

When the chef meets with the hotel's manager about the hives, {{3|he}} usually brings a jar of honey to the meeting. The hotel must also comply {{4|to}} the city's rules for beekeeping: hives must be registered, and inspectors visit twice a year. {{5|Even so,}} the number of rooftop hives in Riverton has tripled since 2015, and the city now counts dozens of beekeepers {{6|who's}} hives sit on roofs and balconies.

{{7|At the present time}}, the hotel sells its honey in the lobby. Guests often say that rooftop honey tastes different from {{8|the countryside}}, and they are right: city bees forage on linden trees, window boxes, and weedy lots, so each season's honey carries a slightly different flavor. {{9|The jars sell for twelve dollars each.}}
